            23:00 (11:00 PM) GMT equals 04:00 AM PKT pre-dawn hours on the next calendar day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.25rem;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--accent);">Example 12: New Year Date Rollover</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.93rem;">
            <strong>23:59 GMT (Dec 31, 2026) → 04:59 PKT (Jan 1, 2027)</strong><br />
            A 23:59 GMT timestamp on New Year's Eve 2026 occurs at 04:59 AM PKT on New Year's Day 2027 in Pakistan!
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What Is GMT? -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>What Is GMT?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong> historically represents mean solar time calculated at the Royal Observatory situated on the Prime Meridian (0° longitude) in Greenwich, London, United Kingdom.
      </p>
      <p>
        Established as an international standard in 1884 at the International Meridian Conference, GMT served for decades as the foundational benchmark against which all global time zones were calibrated. In modern aviation, telecommunications, and computer software architectures, Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) has superseded GMT as the high-precision atomic standard. However, GMT remains the standard civil time zone name for the UK, West Africa, and global conversion tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        Crucially, GMT operates at <strong>UTC+0</strong>. Unlike the United Kingdom's civil time (which shifts to British Summer Time / BST UTC+1 between March and October), standard GMT itself maintains a static zero offset throughout the year. You can learn more about GMT baseline logic on our <a href="/blog/what-is-gmt/" style="color: var(--accent);">What Is GMT guide</a> or explore our primary <a href="/" style="color: var(--accent);">GMT to EST Converter</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What Is PKT? -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>What Is PKT?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Pakistan Standard Time (PKT)</strong>, known natively as پاکستان معیاری وقت (Pakistan Standard Time), is the official time zone governing the Islamic Republic of Pakistan. PKT is defined strictly as <strong>UTC+5</strong> (Coordinated Universal Time plus 5 hours).
      </p>
      <p>
        Centering on 75° East longitude near Islamabad and Lahore, Pakistan Standard Time has been synchronized at UTC+5 since 1951. While daylight saving time experiments were temporarily conducted in 2002 and 2008–2009, Pakistan permanently discontinued DST in October 2009.
      </p>
      <p>
        In global computing and time database systems (the IANA Time Zone Database), Pakistan Standard Time is designated by the location string <code>Asia/Karachi</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Image 2: Pakistan Timezone Visual -->
    <div style="max-width: 750px; margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;">
      <img
        src="/assets/pakistan-standard-time-pkt.webp"
        alt="Pakistan Standard Time PKT UTC plus five timezone illustration"
        width="1200" height="800"
        loading="lazy"
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line);"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- GMT vs PKT Comparison Table -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>GMT vs PKT Comparison</h2>
      <p>
        Review the structural attributes comparing Greenwich Mean Time and Pakistan Standard Time:
      </p>
      <div style="overflow-x: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line);">
        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.95rem;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: var(--panel);">
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">Feature</th>
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</th>
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">Pakistan Standard Time (PKT)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
